lesson reading-the-secret-garden v=1 format=book grade=5 unit=7 subject=reading book=secret-garden levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The Secret Garden"
  goal "Read The Secret Garden and show, with lines from the book, how Mary and Colin change as the garden comes alive."

  sitting chapters=[1, 2, 3] {
    show reading.garden-line k=1 easy-k=0
    show reading.garden-word k=0
    show reading.garden-summary k=0
  }
  sitting chapters=[4, 5] {
    show reading.garden-line k=3 easy-k=2
    show reading.garden-who k=0
    level hard {
      show reading.garden-line k=4
    }
    show reading.garden-narrator k=0
  }
  sitting chapters=[6, 7, 8] {
    show reading.garden-order k=0
    show reading.garden-line k=6 easy-k=5
  }
  sitting chapters=[9, 10, 11] {
    show reading.garden-line k=7 easy-k=8
    level easy {
      show reading.garden-word k=1
    }
    show reading.garden-trust
  }
  sitting chapters=[12, 13] {
    show reading.garden-line k=10 easy-k=9
    show reading.garden-summary k=1
  }
  sitting chapters=[14, 15] {
    show reading.garden-line k=11 easy-k=12
    show reading.garden-who k=1
  }
  sitting chapters=[16, 17, 18] {
    show reading.garden-order k=1
    show reading.garden-line k=13 easy-k=14 hard-k=23
    show reading.garden-shout
  }
  sitting chapters=[19, 20, 21] {
    show reading.garden-line k=16 easy-k=15
    level hard {
      show reading.garden-line k=21
    }
    show reading.garden-summary k=2
  }
  sitting chapters=[22, 23, 24] {
    show reading.garden-line k=17 easy-k=22 hard-k=24
    show reading.garden-secret
  }
  sitting chapters=[25, 26, 27] {
    show reading.garden-line k=19 easy-k=18
    level hard {
      show reading.garden-line k=20
    }
    show reading.garden-narrator k=1
    show reading.garden-change
    show reading.garden-review
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a whole book is read over the term, and that what a reader says about it is
    backed by the book's own lines. Mary arrives at Misselthwaite sour and unloved, finds a garden
    locked for ten years, and brings it and her cousin Colin back to life; every sitting asks for
    the line that proves one step of that, and for a sentence that sums up the part, the order of
    events, a speaker, a word, or what the narrator knows that a character does not. Three questions
    have no single answer and are marked from the list on the grown-ups' sheet. The wrong idea to
    watch for is answering from memory of the story rather than from the page: ask "which line
    shows it?" after every answer, and send the child back to the chapter rather than giving the
    line. If a sitting is too much, read half aloud together and let the child read the rest alone,
    and give a sitting a second day rather than skip its questions. On paper, read any edition from
    a shop or a library; the grown-ups' sheet prints the words of each line beside its number, so
    mark an evidence question by the words the child found. It is the longest of the year's books,
    about 80,000 words, so it has ten sittings, one about every week and a half of the term, at a
    silent pace of about 200 words a minute: chapters 1 to 3 (about 6,000 words, 30 minutes), 4 and
    5 (7,500 words, 38 minutes), 6 to 8 (7,000 words, 35 minutes), 9 to 11 (9,300 words, 46
    minutes), 12 and 13 (6,800 words, 34 minutes), 14 and 15 (7,600 words, 38 minutes), 16 to 18
    (6,800 words, 34 minutes), 19 to 21 (9,300 words, 46 minutes), 22 to 24 (9,500 words, 48
    minutes) and 25 to 27 (10,500 words, 53 minutes, with the review), and any of the longer ones
    can be read over two days. The Yorkshire speech of Martha, Dickon and Ben is written as it sounds;
    reading it aloud helps. The book was written in 1911, and the way it speaks of India and of
    Indian people, and the word it uses for Colin's back, belong to that time: it is worth reading
    those pages together and talking about why they would not be written that way now. Off the
    page, plant something from seed, or find a patch of ground, and keep a diary of it for the rest
    of the term. The book meets Japan's course of study for grades five and six, (3)オ, 日常的に読書に
    親しみ, reading as part of everyday life, and China's 2022 standard for grades five and six,
    阅读与鉴赏 6, 阅读整本书, reading a whole book.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the evidence lines say what the question asks almost in the same words (Mary forgotten, the old key, Dickon keeping secrets, the name of the fox, the tantrum over, Colin's breakfast), and the word wick, which the next line explains, is asked as well."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the evidence lines need interpreting (why Mr Craven shut the garden, that Mary had never heard the truth about herself, that Ben believed Colin was crippled, the lump that comes from fear, the Magic in everyone, the first change in Mr Craven), and three of them are asked on top of the rest."
  }
}
